WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.596 --> 00:00:04.148
- Remind your critics that
an amateur built the arc,

00:00:04.148 --> 00:00:05.939
experts built the Titanic.

00:00:05.939 --> 00:00:08.026
I always loved the game
and my dad loved to play,

00:00:08.026 --> 00:00:09.352
he just taught me to play
the game for the love

00:00:09.352 --> 00:00:11.685
of the game, that's really what I do.

00:00:11.685 --> 00:00:13.573
There are other players
who are more talented,

00:00:13.573 --> 00:00:15.915
but there was no one who
could out prepare me.

00:00:15.915 --> 00:00:18.405
- We all speak regular
Gaelic, but Peyton's kind of

00:00:18.405 --> 00:00:20.429
into his own thing, you know?

00:00:20.429 --> 00:00:23.137
- He's a former American
football quarterback who played

00:00:23.137 --> 00:00:25.307
18 seasons in the NFL.

00:00:25.307 --> 00:00:27.377
He's considered to be one
of the greatest quarterbacks

00:00:27.377 --> 00:00:29.907
of all time, he's the
only starting quarterback

00:00:29.907 --> 00:00:32.942
to win a Super Bowl with
two different franchises.

00:00:32.942 --> 00:00:35.542
He's Peyton Manning, and
here's my take for his top 10

00:00:35.542 --> 00:00:36.820
rules for success.

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:38.732
Rule number one is a personal
favorite, and make sure

00:00:38.732 --> 00:00:40.276
to stick around all the
way to the end for some

00:00:40.276 --> 00:00:41.799
special bonus clips.

00:00:41.799 --> 00:00:43.527
And as always if Peyton says
something that's really,

00:00:43.527 --> 00:00:46.167
really meaningful to you,
please leave it in the comments

00:00:46.167 --> 00:00:48.729
below and put quotes around
it as he's talking so that

00:00:48.729 --> 00:00:51.812
other people can be inspired as well.

00:01:04.363 --> 00:01:07.746
- So don't let expertise silence you.

00:01:07.746 --> 00:01:11.492
Work to find new
solutions to old problems.

00:01:11.492 --> 00:01:13.793
Think outside of the
parameters that restrict

00:01:13.793 --> 00:01:15.696
other people's thoughts.

00:01:15.696 --> 00:01:18.846
Just because you're a novice
on the job, just because

00:01:18.846 --> 00:01:22.254
you haven't faced the same
challenges, just because

00:01:22.254 --> 00:01:26.060
you haven't climbed the
same cliffs, doesn't mean

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:28.611
that you can't contribute to solutions in

00:01:28.611 --> 00:01:30.897
very significant ways.

00:01:30.897 --> 00:01:34.275
When you're chided for your
naivete, and you will be,

00:01:34.275 --> 00:01:38.149
remind your critics that
an amateur built the arc,

00:01:38.149 --> 00:01:40.463
experts built the Titanic.

00:01:40.463 --> 00:01:44.630
I think the fact that that
injury occurred to me has just

00:01:46.071 --> 00:01:49.577
given me more of an
appreciation for this journey

00:01:49.577 --> 00:01:53.446
that I'm on and I have had
fun this entire playoff.

00:01:53.446 --> 00:01:57.613
I've tried to soak it up, I
haven't just been so laser

00:01:58.447 --> 00:02:00.860
focused where I haven't enjoyed anything.

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:05.439
We'e had two good post
playoff win celebrations,

00:02:05.439 --> 00:02:08.797
gave it a good run after
San Diego and a really good

00:02:08.797 --> 00:02:12.964
run after New England, and I
think that's some advice that

00:02:13.983 --> 00:02:18.150
some players in their older
years gave to me and I really

00:02:19.183 --> 00:02:20.634
tried to follow that.

00:02:20.634 --> 00:02:23.262
And I plan to enjoy this week as well.

00:02:23.262 --> 00:02:25.721
We had a great week of preparation
last week, it's important

00:02:25.721 --> 00:02:30.520
this week, but I do think
it's important to soak it up

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:32.750
and as they say "enjoy the journey not

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:34.422
"just the destination."

00:02:34.422 --> 00:02:37.261
I learned a valuable lesson
when I was a freshman

00:02:37.261 --> 00:02:41.076
at Tennessee that I
applied to my first year on

00:02:41.076 --> 00:02:42.745
the job in the NFL.

00:02:42.745 --> 00:02:44.824
It was the first time I
ran into the huddle as

00:02:44.824 --> 00:02:46.755
a quarterback in Tennessee.

00:02:46.755 --> 00:02:50.160
We were playing up at UCLA
at the Rose Bowl, 95,000

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:53.265
people in the stands, ABC
broadcasting the game on

00:02:53.265 --> 00:02:56.835
national television, Keith
Jackson and Bob Greasi.

00:02:56.835 --> 00:02:59.570
Tennessee was ranked ninth
in the country, UCLA was

00:02:59.570 --> 00:03:02.484
unranked, expected to be a blowout.

00:03:02.484 --> 00:03:05.156
I was 13 on the depth
chart not expecting to play

00:03:05.156 --> 00:03:08.097
the entire game much
less the entire season.

00:03:08.097 --> 00:03:10.245
On the second play of the
game our starting quarterback

00:03:10.245 --> 00:03:12.864
tears his knee, he's out for the year.

00:03:12.864 --> 00:03:15.551
Our backup quarterback was
a guy named Todd Helton

00:03:15.551 --> 00:03:18.542
who went onto have an 18
year major league baseball

00:03:18.542 --> 00:03:21.047
career, let's just say Todd
was kind of thinking about

00:03:21.047 --> 00:03:23.811
that baseball signing
bonus he was about to get,

00:03:23.811 --> 00:03:27.080
he wasn't real crazy
about going into the game.

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:29.165
So we're getting to be 21 to
nothing, my coach turns to

00:03:29.165 --> 00:03:31.291
me and says "Peyton, you're going in."

00:03:31.291 --> 00:03:33.300
And boy I didn't think I was
nervous, I looked down and all

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:35.969
the hair on my arms was
juts sticking straight up.

00:03:35.969 --> 00:03:38.152
So I got into that huddle, and
I remembered something that

00:03:38.152 --> 00:03:41.186
my daddy told me, he said
"son if you ever get into the

00:03:41.186 --> 00:03:43.671
"huddle with the stars at
any point in the season,

00:03:43.671 --> 00:03:46.059
"it may be in the fourth
quarter blowout, it may be just

00:03:46.059 --> 00:03:48.624
"in practice, it doesn't
matter, you be the leader

00:03:48.624 --> 00:03:50.637
"and you take control of that huddle.

00:03:50.637 --> 00:03:52.991
"That's your job as the
quarterback, you're just 18

00:03:52.991 --> 00:03:56.285
"years old, most of these
seniors are 21, 22, it doesn't

00:03:56.285 --> 00:03:59.477
"matter, be the leader and
take control of that huddle."

00:03:59.477 --> 00:04:02.983
So I remembered old Dad's advice
and I get into that huddle,

00:04:02.983 --> 00:04:05.219
and I said "alright guys,
I know I'm just a freshman

00:04:05.219 --> 00:04:07.336
"but I can take us down the
field right now, get us a

00:04:07.336 --> 00:04:10.223
"touchdown, get us back
in this game, let's go!"

00:04:10.223 --> 00:04:12.735
Big left tackle, a guy named
Jason Layman, about six

00:04:12.735 --> 00:04:16.185
five, 330 pounds, grabs me
by the shoulder and says

00:04:16.185 --> 00:04:17.987
"hey freshman, shut the blank up and

00:04:17.987 --> 00:04:19.557
"call the blanking play."

00:04:19.557 --> 00:04:21.807
(laughing)

00:04:24.415 --> 00:04:26.165
And I said "yes sir."

00:04:29.506 --> 00:04:31.095
That was really great advice from my dad,

00:04:31.095 --> 00:04:33.699
I really appreciate that.

00:04:33.699 --> 00:04:35.528
Well I've always had a passion
for the game, but when you

00:04:35.528 --> 00:04:39.333
are out for a year you
certainly miss certain things.

00:04:39.333 --> 00:04:44.239
Being in that huddle, flying
back on the plane after

00:04:44.239 --> 00:04:49.149
a big victory, the weight
room and after practice

00:04:49.149 --> 00:04:51.537
throwing sessions with your
receivers, they're going to

00:04:51.537 --> 00:04:54.001
make a difference and help
you win a game on Sunday.

00:04:54.001 --> 00:04:56.603
You just don't get to
participate in those activities

00:04:56.603 --> 00:04:58.374
when you're not playing
and you're injured.

00:04:58.374 --> 00:05:01.969
So to get back out on the
field I had more of an

00:05:01.969 --> 00:05:04.437
appreciation for the little
things, everybody enjoys

00:05:04.437 --> 00:05:06.542
playing football in the
games, but I have more of

00:05:06.542 --> 00:05:09.108
an appreciation for the small
details, the things that you

00:05:09.108 --> 00:05:12.289
won't get to do after you
stop playing football.

00:05:12.289 --> 00:05:16.366
So those moments have
been even more special.

00:05:16.366 --> 00:05:20.684
In my very first NFL game I
completed my first pass to

00:05:20.684 --> 00:05:23.476
hall of fame running back Marshall Fall.

00:05:23.476 --> 00:05:26.292
I threw a touch down in that
same game to Marvin Harrison,

00:05:26.292 --> 00:05:29.826
who would inducted into the
hall of fame this August.

00:05:29.826 --> 00:05:32.867
The quarterback for our
opponent, the Miami Dolphins

00:05:32.867 --> 00:05:36.169
was after my dad, my favorite player,

00:05:36.169 --> 00:05:39.847
hall-of-famer Dan Marino,
who won the first third

00:05:39.847 --> 00:05:43.820
down of the game, completed
a 25 yard skinny post,

00:05:43.820 --> 00:05:46.626
and it was the damnedest
throw I've ever seen.

00:05:46.626 --> 00:05:50.636
Later I completed a pass
to tight end Marcus Pollard

00:05:50.636 --> 00:05:54.490
down the middle, and somebody
hit me really hard and after

00:05:54.490 --> 00:05:58.657
I got up I told myself "I know
I can play in this league."

00:05:59.628 --> 00:06:02.421
Later in that struggling
season, we played in and lost

00:06:02.421 --> 00:06:05.170
to Baltimore, it was the
first time that the Colts had

00:06:05.170 --> 00:06:09.337
returned to Baltimore since
they had moved in 1984.

00:06:10.565 --> 00:06:13.650
We didn't exactly get a
warm reception that day.

00:06:13.650 --> 00:06:15.581
Fans were screaming at
me and I was thinking

00:06:15.581 --> 00:06:19.716
"hey I was only eight years
old then, get off of my back."

00:06:19.716 --> 00:06:22.321
I had met him once before
but when the game was over

00:06:22.321 --> 00:06:25.675
I had a chance to shake
Johnny Unitas's hand.

00:06:25.675 --> 00:06:30.048
He told me "Peyton, you stay
at it, I'm pulling for you."

00:06:30.048 --> 00:06:34.657
Well, I have stayed at it,
I've stayed at it for 18 years.

00:06:34.657 --> 00:06:38.824
And I hope that old number 19
is up there with his flat top

00:06:39.981 --> 00:06:43.120
and maybe his black high tops on.

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:46.552
And I hope he knows that I
have stayed at it and maybe

00:06:46.552 --> 00:06:49.574
he's even a little proud of me.

00:06:49.574 --> 00:06:51.095
- Because now it's all
about money, it's all

00:06:51.095 --> 00:06:52.630
"let's get out and play
and make the millions,"

00:06:52.630 --> 00:06:54.913
because you don't know what
could happen in that final year.

00:06:54.913 --> 00:06:56.468
- Right, that's kind of the
norm now, people take the

00:06:56.468 --> 00:07:00.605
money and go pro, but just
growing up around college

00:07:00.605 --> 00:07:03.323
football I always loved the
game, and my dad loved to play.

00:07:03.323 --> 00:07:05.401
He juts taught me to play
for the love of the game,

00:07:05.401 --> 00:07:07.165
and that's really what I do.

00:07:07.165 --> 00:07:08.721
I like to play the game
and I want to come back and

00:07:08.721 --> 00:07:09.835
play another year of college football.

00:07:09.835 --> 00:07:10.668
- [David] Well good for you.

00:07:10.668 --> 00:07:13.998
- And I told our editors Chris
Stone and Paul Ficktenbaum

00:07:13.998 --> 00:07:16.693
a story about last year after
one of the most crushing

00:07:16.693 --> 00:07:20.452
defeats of your career,
right here in this stadium

00:07:20.452 --> 00:07:23.094
against the Baltimore
Ravens, that you waited for

00:07:23.094 --> 00:07:25.464
about 15 minutes afterwards,
with your wife and with

00:07:25.464 --> 00:07:28.888
your son for Ray Lewis to come out.

00:07:28.888 --> 00:07:30.387
Why'd you do that?

00:07:30.387 --> 00:07:34.220
- Well Ray Lewis has been
the greatest defensive player

00:07:34.220 --> 00:07:36.779
that I've ever played against,
and I knew that was going to

00:07:36.779 --> 00:07:40.371
be his last game and you just
don't cross paths with those

00:07:40.371 --> 00:07:42.946
players once they stop playing.

00:07:42.946 --> 00:07:47.546
So it was definitely a
disappointing night but I thought

00:07:47.546 --> 00:07:49.998
it was the appropriate thing
to do to go over and pay

00:07:49.998 --> 00:07:52.154
respect to one of the greatest
defensive players of all

00:07:52.154 --> 00:07:54.951
time and the best player of my era.

00:07:54.951 --> 00:07:57.655
And took the moment and
I was glad that I did it.

00:07:57.655 --> 00:08:01.335
I played the Ravens this
year, it wasn't the same not

00:08:01.335 --> 00:08:04.228
seeing number 52 over there,
I haven't seen him since

00:08:04.228 --> 00:08:06.954
that moment and realized
that I won't see him quite

00:08:06.954 --> 00:08:11.121
as often, so I guess that was
an example of sportsmanship,

00:08:12.270 --> 00:08:14.886
it certainly wasn't easy,
I can promise you that.

00:08:14.886 --> 00:08:17.485
But I was glad that I
did it because I thought

00:08:17.485 --> 00:08:19.134
he deserved my respects.

00:08:19.134 --> 00:08:21.718
I look back on my NFL career,
I know without a doubt

00:08:21.718 --> 00:08:24.048
that I gave everything I
had to help my teams walk

00:08:24.048 --> 00:08:25.381
away with a win.

00:08:26.453 --> 00:08:29.085
There were other players who
were more talented, but there

00:08:29.085 --> 00:08:32.227
was no one who could out prepare me.

00:08:32.227 --> 00:08:35.823
And because of that I have no regrets.

00:08:35.823 --> 00:08:38.560
There's a scripture
reading, second Timothy 4:7,

00:08:38.560 --> 00:08:40.606
"I have fought the good
fight and I have finished

00:08:40.606 --> 00:08:43.252
"the race, I have kept the faith."

00:08:43.252 --> 00:08:47.557
Well I have fought a good
fight, I've finished my football

00:08:47.557 --> 00:08:50.474
race, and after 18 years it's time.

00:08:51.878 --> 00:08:55.498
God bless all of you,
and God bless football.

00:08:55.498 --> 00:08:59.832
A hard hit or not, I don't
believe there's any way to

00:08:59.832 --> 00:09:02.796
overprepare for your future.

00:09:02.796 --> 00:09:06.042
Have a formidable plan,
have a backup plan,

00:09:06.042 --> 00:09:09.130
and have a back up to your backup plan.

00:09:09.130 --> 00:09:12.589
My preparation began by
watching game tape of our

00:09:12.589 --> 00:09:15.112
team and competitors.

00:09:15.112 --> 00:09:18.190
It helps me put the pieces
of the puzzle together.

00:09:18.190 --> 00:09:20.788
Years ago I had a system put
in my house where I could

00:09:20.788 --> 00:09:23.994
analyze every game, every
competitor, without having

00:09:23.994 --> 00:09:27.188
to go into the team's practice facility.

00:09:27.188 --> 00:09:30.981
So if you ask me how long do
I watch game film to prepare,

00:09:30.981 --> 00:09:34.174
I couldn't tell you because
I never kept a clock.

00:09:34.174 --> 00:09:39.136
I just watch until I feel
ready, I watch and I prepare.

00:09:39.136 --> 00:09:42.140
I can actually get lost
in the intense focus of

00:09:42.140 --> 00:09:46.307
figuring things out that
other may never even look at.

00:09:47.144 --> 00:09:50.498
If you really want to be
a game changer out there,

00:09:50.498 --> 00:09:53.226
become a master observer.

00:09:53.226 --> 00:09:56.410
Just the willingness to
stop and look at things that

00:09:56.410 --> 00:10:00.152
no one else has even bothered
to look at, enables game

00:10:00.152 --> 00:10:04.252
changers to identify
real points of meaning.

00:10:04.252 --> 00:10:07.991
The simple process of focusing
on things that are normally

00:10:07.991 --> 00:10:10.963
taken for granted, can
be a source of great

00:10:10.963 --> 00:10:12.713
power and creativity.

00:10:14.098 --> 00:10:16.442
I'm a naturally curious
person, I want to know and

00:10:16.442 --> 00:10:18.859
thoroughly understand things.

00:10:19.817 --> 00:10:23.204
We learned and we observed,
we'd pick up on clues,

00:10:23.204 --> 00:10:27.371
we figure things out, that is
what preparation is all about.

00:10:28.744 --> 00:10:30.994
(shouting)

00:10:31.963 --> 00:10:34.005
- [Reporter] Manning uses
a rare form of Gaelic at

00:10:34.005 --> 00:10:35.597
the line of scrimmage.

00:10:35.597 --> 00:10:37.847
(shouting)

00:10:39.755 --> 00:10:41.643
- I mean we all speak
regular Gaelic, but Peyton's

00:10:41.643 --> 00:10:43.614
kind of into his own thing, you know?

00:10:43.614 --> 00:10:45.260
Takes me forever to translate it.

00:10:45.260 --> 00:10:47.510
(shouting)

00:10:48.466 --> 00:10:50.572
- How do you decipher any of it?

00:10:50.572 --> 00:10:52.196
- Well he's been sticking
his hand up my butt

00:10:52.196 --> 00:10:55.181
for 11 years, so we're pretty close.

00:10:55.181 --> 00:10:57.431
(shouting)

00:10:59.373 --> 00:11:00.948
- My brother Cooper
actually is the one who

00:11:00.948 --> 00:11:01.893
taught me Gaelic.

00:11:01.893 --> 00:11:05.161
He's really into it, he's got
a personalized license plate.

00:11:05.161 --> 00:11:08.161
- What does it say "Mr.
Gaelic" or something?

00:11:08.161 --> 00:11:11.628
- Why you making fun of my brother?

00:11:11.628 --> 00:11:13.843
This Gaelic stuff's awesome
but I like to mix it up

00:11:13.843 --> 00:11:14.778
every now and then.

00:11:14.778 --> 00:11:17.622
A lot of times I just say
whatever comes to mind.

00:11:17.622 --> 00:11:18.936
Bacon bacon bacon!

00:11:18.936 --> 00:11:20.150
Hot sauce, hot sauce!

00:11:20.150 --> 00:11:21.543
Taco taco!

00:11:21.543 --> 00:11:23.422
- I don't know what that means.

00:11:23.422 --> 00:11:24.774
- [Peyton] Bacon bacon bacon!

00:11:24.774 --> 00:11:26.217
- I don't know what the
guy's talking about most

00:11:26.217 --> 00:11:28.349
of the time, I just run
an out route and just hope

00:11:28.349 --> 00:11:29.751
he throws it to me.

00:11:29.751 --> 00:11:31.531
- Chili chili chili!

00:11:31.531 --> 00:11:32.364
- What?!

00:11:32.364 --> 00:11:33.898
- Taco, taco, taco!

00:11:33.898 --> 00:11:36.590
A lot of times Kenny, we
have no idea what we're doing

00:11:36.590 --> 00:11:40.187
but the defense doesn't
know that we don't know what

00:11:40.187 --> 00:11:43.405
we're doing, and that's next level.

00:11:43.405 --> 00:11:45.655
(shouting)

00:11:55.437 --> 00:11:57.604
Ice cream alert, 654 Jose!

00:11:59.536 --> 00:12:01.239
Okay, or nothing!

00:12:01.239 --> 00:12:03.216
- [Reporter Two] Peyton
Manning and the Colts, from the

00:12:03.216 --> 00:12:06.123
NFL's most sophisticated offense.

00:12:06.123 --> 00:12:10.724
Each play can be called
or changed at the line.

00:12:10.724 --> 00:12:14.641
That forces the Colt's
lineman to wait and wait

00:12:16.101 --> 00:12:19.097
until Peyton finally makes up his mind.

00:12:19.097 --> 00:12:23.897
I wonder if they ever think
"just snap the damn ball!"

00:12:23.897 --> 00:12:26.513
- Check brown, Richmond,
96 double 96 double!

00:12:26.513 --> 00:12:28.513
Run a flare run a flare!

00:12:29.942 --> 00:12:32.239
- [Mike] His goal's not
to get the perfect play,

00:12:32.239 --> 00:12:34.815
it's to get out of a bad play.

00:12:34.815 --> 00:12:36.495
And more times than not they do that.

00:12:36.495 --> 00:12:38.527
- [Announcer] Peyton drops,
look, fire, touchdown

00:12:38.527 --> 00:12:39.777
making it rain!

00:12:41.394 --> 00:12:44.584
- It's a ballet that drives
people crazy, I just say

00:12:44.584 --> 00:12:46.255
"just snap the damn ball!"

00:12:46.255 --> 00:12:47.721
They'll do it when
they're damn well pleased.

00:12:47.721 --> 00:12:49.971
(shouting)

00:12:54.647 --> 00:12:57.823
- It started way back I guess
kind of around my second

00:12:57.823 --> 00:13:00.667
year with Tom Moore, we kind
of just sat down and put

00:13:00.667 --> 00:13:03.556
some code words together
and things that kind of made

00:13:03.556 --> 00:13:05.567
sense, but it has taken off from that.

00:13:05.567 --> 00:13:08.865
First right, shore locks,
shore locks, shore locks!

00:13:08.865 --> 00:13:10.358
Richmond, Florida, Buffalo!

00:13:10.358 --> 00:13:11.191
37 double!

00:13:12.670 --> 00:13:14.951
Louisville soul train,
Louisville soul train!

00:13:14.951 --> 00:13:17.451
Purple, purple Buddha raiders!

00:13:18.322 --> 00:13:20.187
It's not about trickery.

00:13:20.187 --> 00:13:22.112
Purple Buddha raiders!

00:13:22.112 --> 00:13:25.134
But it does take constat
communication because when you

00:13:25.134 --> 00:13:29.301
do change a pass play to a
run, a run play to a pass play,

00:13:30.552 --> 00:13:33.055
it's not easy as just, hey let's throw it.

00:13:33.055 --> 00:13:36.345
It's I say something and
then you tell the offensive

00:13:36.345 --> 00:13:38.898
line, they have an
entirely different language

00:13:38.898 --> 00:13:41.898
that really nobody else understands.

00:13:45.349 --> 00:13:48.554
It's unbelievable what
these guys go through.

00:13:48.554 --> 00:13:51.387
Thinking about go ahead and block.

00:13:57.217 --> 00:14:00.377
(screaming)

00:14:00.377 --> 00:14:02.642
If you don't have linemen
that are smart, that can

00:14:02.642 --> 00:14:04.918
like I said, just comprehend
it all with five seconds,

00:14:04.918 --> 00:14:08.335
make their calls, call
east, west, slug, dumbo,

00:14:08.335 --> 00:14:10.720
you name it, these words
that, you know what do these

00:14:10.720 --> 00:14:12.295
words come from, what
other business requires

00:14:12.295 --> 00:14:14.128
using words like that?

00:14:15.108 --> 00:14:18.275
(saying random words)

00:14:22.561 --> 00:14:26.049
- Bonanza, I mean that
really doesn't mean anything

00:14:26.049 --> 00:14:29.002
but could be a call that
we use, I don't know.

00:14:29.002 --> 00:14:30.534
(laughing)

00:14:30.534 --> 00:14:32.568
- Could be, maybe not, not sure.

00:14:32.568 --> 00:14:36.120
(speaking jibberish)

00:14:36.120 --> 00:14:39.370
- It's not so much the coach
that needs to know what

00:14:39.370 --> 00:14:41.370
we're talking about, it's
the players that need to talk

00:14:41.370 --> 00:14:45.094
to one another, so we leave
a lot of their code words

00:14:45.094 --> 00:14:47.823
and calls and that sort
of thing up to them.

00:14:47.823 --> 00:14:50.649
That means that you may
have to do a scat right,

00:14:50.649 --> 00:14:52.732
255, brip brip, scoop it.

00:14:53.668 --> 00:14:55.333
- Hold on before you get in
there, we have to call bogus

00:14:55.333 --> 00:14:58.136
on Howard Mudd saying he's not invovled.

00:14:58.136 --> 00:15:00.886
- I said don't talk, let me talk!

00:15:01.795 --> 00:15:03.453
- I'm glad somebody spoke up.

00:15:03.453 --> 00:15:06.018
- That man is involved in
every part of every call

00:15:06.018 --> 00:15:07.306
we will ever make.

00:15:07.306 --> 00:15:09.005
- 51 made the play,
you know why, because--

00:15:09.005 --> 00:15:12.322
- 93 made the play, 51 made the--

00:15:12.322 --> 00:15:14.003
- That's exactly what
happened, I was right there!

00:15:14.003 --> 00:15:16.532
- They need to be smart
and they need to be tough

00:15:16.532 --> 00:15:19.694
minded, tough minded in that
they may only get a piece

00:15:19.694 --> 00:15:22.535
of the information or they didn't get

00:15:22.535 --> 00:15:23.611
any of the information.

00:15:23.611 --> 00:15:26.084
- I remember in Kansas
City in the playoff game,

00:15:26.084 --> 00:15:28.024
the left, neither of us knew
the play and we couldn't

00:15:28.024 --> 00:15:31.541
hear, we just kind of ran
out and it turned out great.

00:15:31.541 --> 00:15:32.915
- [Announcer] Hughes
goes off the left side,

00:15:32.915 --> 00:15:34.721
cuts it up the middle,
and gets to the end zone!

00:15:34.721 --> 00:15:35.638
Touch down!

00:15:36.653 --> 00:15:40.408
- The way we talk about
it is you have to hear.

00:15:40.408 --> 00:15:41.796
There's not an option "I didn't hear ya."

00:15:41.796 --> 00:15:44.888
And if somebody doesn't hear,
I really feel responsible

00:15:44.888 --> 00:15:46.834
and so that's why I
always kind of go back for

00:15:46.834 --> 00:15:48.821
a second time, I go down
the line just to make sure

00:15:48.821 --> 00:15:51.813
everybody knows what to do,
because if one guy doesn't

00:15:51.813 --> 00:15:55.980
hear, that guy misses the
block and you have a bad play.

00:15:58.160 --> 00:15:59.952
Water or green light?

00:15:59.952 --> 00:16:00.785
- Either one.

00:16:00.785 --> 00:16:03.151
- Green light, let's go green light.

00:16:03.151 --> 00:16:05.074
- You can read through it
in your play book as much as

00:16:05.074 --> 00:16:07.605
you want, but you're not
going to understand it until

00:16:07.605 --> 00:16:10.263
you're out there on the field
actually hearing it come

00:16:10.263 --> 00:16:11.375
out of the quarterback's mouth.

00:16:11.375 --> 00:16:15.494
- Chin chin, point left, point left, 55!

00:16:15.494 --> 00:16:17.444
- [Coach] There are times
when he calls the real play

00:16:17.444 --> 00:16:18.594
and that changes it.

00:16:18.594 --> 00:16:21.670
- Good job, Japan, Japan, Japan!

00:16:21.670 --> 00:16:22.555
Japan Japan!

00:16:22.555 --> 00:16:25.827
- There are times when
he calls a phony play.

00:16:25.827 --> 00:16:28.830
(shouting)

00:16:28.830 --> 00:16:32.472
To extract a reaction
before we call a real play.

00:16:32.472 --> 00:16:35.389
- Lop lop, buffalo, lop buffalo 14!

00:16:36.465 --> 00:16:39.489
- Then we have the fake play real play,

00:16:39.489 --> 00:16:41.014
and then change the real play.

00:16:41.014 --> 00:16:42.847
Sally 19, 19 Sally 19!

00:16:45.388 --> 00:16:46.221
19 Sally!

00:16:48.528 --> 00:16:51.075
- I like that he's changing
the play, because when he's

00:16:51.075 --> 00:16:53.503
changing the play the defense
sees us looking at him

00:16:53.503 --> 00:16:55.620
or looking at Jeff or
whoever, and they're just

00:16:55.620 --> 00:16:57.927
as confused, I mean they
don't know when the ball's

00:16:57.927 --> 00:17:01.064
going to be snapped, so a lot
of times they kind of relax

00:17:01.064 --> 00:17:05.231
a little bit and wait for us
to get done communicating.

00:17:06.412 --> 00:17:07.851
- They did a lot of guessing.

00:17:07.851 --> 00:17:12.018
I mean they're trying to
guess what play we're running.

00:17:15.027 --> 00:17:18.000
Listening to our calls,
oh when they do this this

00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:19.397
is what they do.

00:17:19.397 --> 00:17:21.822
- When they're behind his legs,

00:17:21.822 --> 00:17:23.226
he's giving the finger first!

00:17:23.226 --> 00:17:24.878
- Yeah he's giving them how
many times he's going to do it.

00:17:24.878 --> 00:17:29.045
- [Tomas] When his leg goes
down, that ball is coming.

00:17:31.033 --> 00:17:33.417
- If I were a defender,
listening to our calls wouldn't

00:17:33.417 --> 00:17:35.760
be one of the big things for
me, because he's just one

00:17:35.760 --> 00:17:38.442
of those guys that
he'll fake it or he does

00:17:38.442 --> 00:17:42.354
his little gymnastics back
there and defenses bite.

00:17:42.354 --> 00:17:45.878
So the whole "listen to the
words" hasn't worked yet.

00:17:45.878 --> 00:17:50.290
- Fly left, fly left,
hippies mic, fly left!

00:17:50.290 --> 00:17:52.762
- Really we don't get to
see a lot of of him because

00:17:52.762 --> 00:17:54.742
he's orchestrating behind us.

00:17:54.742 --> 00:17:57.565
- Behind the buffalo, hind to buffalo.

00:17:57.565 --> 00:18:00.129
- But we hear from other
people like family or

00:18:00.129 --> 00:18:01.949
friends, like "what is that!?"

00:18:01.949 --> 00:18:04.532
- Apple apple inside, 54 54 54!

00:18:08.888 --> 00:18:11.456
I think about quarterbacks,
their defining legacy,

00:18:11.456 --> 00:18:15.759
you think about Dan Marino,
it's just the quick release.

00:18:15.759 --> 00:18:19.354
From Ellway it's the
sprint right throw back

00:18:19.354 --> 00:18:20.687
across the left.

00:18:21.717 --> 00:18:24.098
Montana, it's the classic
throwing motion and

00:18:24.098 --> 00:18:25.931
the two minute drives.

00:18:29.577 --> 00:18:33.715
For me I think it's going
to be this, picking my nose,

00:18:33.715 --> 00:18:36.042
ear, you know, this, all
these different things

00:18:36.042 --> 00:18:37.166
that I do.

00:18:37.166 --> 00:18:39.180
I don't know if I want that
to be my legacy but that

00:18:39.180 --> 00:18:42.265
is what we have to do and
you're constantly having

00:18:42.265 --> 00:18:44.684
to do something, either
tell the center something,

00:18:44.684 --> 00:18:47.171
throw the tight ends, tell
the receiver, tell the backs.

00:18:47.171 --> 00:18:48.684
It's what is required
of the quarterback to

00:18:48.684 --> 00:18:50.853
get things communicated.

00:18:50.853 --> 00:18:53.603
- [Mike] It's an amazing
compilation of people and schemes

00:18:53.603 --> 00:18:54.847
and it works.

00:18:54.847 --> 00:18:56.175
- Five 28!

00:18:56.175 --> 00:18:58.285
- People say "just stamp the
damn ball" but again if you

00:18:58.285 --> 00:19:00.636
look at the traffic since
Peyton's been there, it's been

00:19:00.636 --> 00:19:03.637
a great orchestration that's worked.

00:19:03.637 --> 00:19:05.717
- [Reporter Two] Because the
Colts can be more reactive at

00:19:05.717 --> 00:19:09.717
the line than other teams,
they take fewer risks

00:19:10.999 --> 00:19:14.563
and score more touch downs,
and perhaps that will

00:19:14.563 --> 00:19:18.511
be the legacy of Peyton
Manning and his offensive line.

00:19:18.511 --> 00:19:19.999
- [Announcer Two] Boy the
Colts are looking tremendous,

00:19:19.999 --> 00:19:22.292
protection was excellent.

00:19:22.292 --> 00:19:24.792
(celebrating)

00:19:25.699 --> 00:19:28.412
- Thank you guys so much for
watching, I hope you enjoyed.

00:19:28.412 --> 00:19:31.687
I made this video because
Arturo Menja asked me to.

00:19:31.687 --> 00:19:33.580
So if there's a famous
entrepreneur that you want me

00:19:33.580 --> 00:19:36.181
to profile next, leave it
down in the comments below

00:19:36.181 --> 00:19:37.669
and I'll see what I can do.

00:19:37.669 --> 00:19:40.150
I'd also love to know, what
did you learn from Peyton

00:19:40.150 --> 00:19:41.263
today, what was your favorite
clip, what message was most

00:19:41.263 --> 00:19:43.434
meaningful to you, what are
you going to apply to your

00:19:43.434 --> 00:19:45.979
business, leave it in the
comments below and I'm going to

00:19:45.979 --> 00:19:47.436
join in the discussion.

00:19:47.436 --> 00:19:49.914
Finally, I want to give a
quick shout out to Ben Stucke,

00:19:49.914 --> 00:19:52.331
Ben thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book.

00:19:52.331 --> 00:19:54.573
It really really really means a lot to me.

00:19:54.573 --> 00:19:57.343
So thank you guys again for
watching, I believe in you.

00:19:57.343 --> 00:19:59.612
I hope you continue to
believe in yourselves.

00:19:59.612 --> 00:20:03.779
And whatever your one word is,
much love, I'll see you soon.

00:20:10.343 --> 00:20:12.685
- I thought it was very
interesting when I saw you here

00:20:12.685 --> 00:20:14.937
at training camp this summer,
one of the things I said

00:20:14.937 --> 00:20:17.472
to you, I asked you a question,
what do you think you'll

00:20:17.472 --> 00:20:19.634
be doing in 15 years?

00:20:19.634 --> 00:20:22.335
And you wouldn't answer the
question, and I thought it

00:20:22.335 --> 00:20:23.431
was so interesting.

00:20:23.431 --> 00:20:25.792
Why wouldn't you answer that question?

00:20:25.792 --> 00:20:28.633
- Well I think maybe had you
asked me two years ago I might

00:20:28.633 --> 00:20:32.719
have had more of an idea but
like I said, these past two

00:20:32.719 --> 00:20:36.940
years have truly changed
my life, and so I'm really

00:20:36.940 --> 00:20:39.283
at a new place and kind of
in the second chapter of

00:20:39.283 --> 00:20:43.067
my football career, and
I'm taking that chapter one

00:20:43.067 --> 00:20:46.443
page at a time, and so it
feels so good to be back out

00:20:46.443 --> 00:20:50.215
on the field playing this
great game, and I realized

00:20:50.215 --> 00:20:53.097
after that injury, I think
I always have realized this,

00:20:53.097 --> 00:20:56.048
but it was certainly reinforced
that next year certainly

00:20:56.048 --> 00:21:00.121
is not promised, so I am
all in for 2013 and I'm

00:21:00.121 --> 00:21:02.538
certainly enjoying this year.

00:21:04.522 --> 00:21:09.155
♫ Crawfish shorts I like your style

00:21:09.155 --> 00:21:11.526
♫ Hooked it just a little bit

00:21:11.526 --> 00:21:14.718
(shattering glass)

00:21:14.718 --> 00:21:18.551
♫ Don't open that cellar door

00:21:20.364 --> 00:21:24.531
♫ Epic comeback starts right here

00:21:26.631 --> 00:21:27.548
Lucky shot.

00:21:28.398 --> 00:21:32.821
- [Narrator] He wore the number
18 but he is the number one

00:21:32.821 --> 00:21:35.750
in all time passing yards.

00:21:35.750 --> 00:21:39.982
The number one in all passing touch downs.

00:21:39.982 --> 00:21:41.386
And the number one in--

00:21:41.386 --> 00:21:44.191
♫ This smoke machine is really cool

00:21:44.191 --> 00:21:46.946
- [Narrator] Yeah it's pretty great.

00:21:46.946 --> 00:21:49.279
- Can I get some more smoke?

00:21:52.018 --> 00:21:52.851
Perfect.

00:21:55.867 --> 00:21:57.617
- Shall we say grace?

00:21:58.538 --> 00:21:59.810
Heavenly Father.

00:21:59.810 --> 00:22:03.477
- [Narrator Two] She
had the perfect family.

00:22:06.569 --> 00:22:08.433
An adoring husband.

00:22:08.433 --> 00:22:09.655
- Why you smiling?

00:22:09.655 --> 00:22:12.263
- I don't know, I'm just happy.

00:22:12.263 --> 00:22:15.826
- [Narrator Two] And an ideal life.

00:22:15.826 --> 00:22:18.414
(laughing)

00:22:18.414 --> 00:22:20.997
But she also had a dark secret.

00:22:22.491 --> 00:22:24.408
She liked to collect...

00:22:26.056 --> 00:22:27.473
Football players.

00:22:30.918 --> 00:22:34.537
- Do you have any place to stay tonight?

00:22:34.537 --> 00:22:36.437
- Yes I have a place to stay.

00:22:36.437 --> 00:22:37.270
- Don't you dare lie to me.

00:22:37.270 --> 00:22:38.737
- Lady I'm not lying, I'm Peyton Manning

00:22:38.737 --> 00:22:40.737
I own a couple of homes.

00:22:48.573 --> 00:22:49.911
- Come on.

00:22:49.911 --> 00:22:51.980
- Fine, but only because
you're freaking me out.

00:22:51.980 --> 00:22:53.453
- [Narrator Two] Now this quarterback...

00:22:53.453 --> 00:22:55.180
- Oh and Sean says all the
pro athletes use futons

00:22:55.180 --> 00:22:57.277
if they don't find a bed big enough.

00:22:57.277 --> 00:22:58.439
I got you something nicer.

00:22:58.439 --> 00:23:00.625
- [Narrator Two] Is in a
game of life and death.

00:23:00.625 --> 00:23:01.921
- I never had one before.

00:23:01.921 --> 00:23:03.495
- What, a room to yourself?

00:23:03.495 --> 00:23:07.734
- No, a crazy lady holding
me against my will.

00:23:07.734 --> 00:23:09.651
- Wel you have one now.

00:23:12.279 --> 00:23:14.055
- [Narrator Two] Starring academy award

00:23:14.055 --> 00:23:15.888
winner Sandra Bullock.

00:23:19.736 --> 00:23:20.998
Mike?

00:23:20.998 --> 00:23:22.875
- Eli, this is serious.

00:23:22.875 --> 00:23:25.793
- [Narrator Two] And four
time NFL MVP Peyton Manning.

00:23:25.793 --> 00:23:30.287
- This crazy lady is holding
me prisoner in her house.

00:23:30.287 --> 00:23:31.204
- Big Mike?

00:23:33.148 --> 00:23:35.250
- She keeps calling me "big Mike!"

00:23:35.250 --> 00:23:37.010
Stop laughing, it's not funny.

00:23:37.010 --> 00:23:38.993
- [Narrator Two] Was
she crazy about football

00:23:38.993 --> 00:23:40.517
or just crazy?

00:23:40.517 --> 00:23:42.245
- That's another thing,
we need to find more

00:23:42.245 --> 00:23:43.719
about his past.

00:23:43.719 --> 00:23:45.409
- But he won't talk about
it, he's like an onion

00:23:45.409 --> 00:23:47.888
you have to peel him
back a layer at a time

00:23:47.888 --> 00:23:49.971
- Not if you use a knife.

00:23:51.415 --> 00:23:54.352
- [Narrator Two] She wouldn't
let anyone get in her way.

00:23:54.352 --> 00:23:57.227
- Michael needs a B,
what does he have to do

00:23:57.227 --> 00:23:59.162
to deserve that?

00:23:59.162 --> 00:24:01.713
- First of all his name's
not Michael and he is very

00:24:01.713 --> 00:24:04.639
clearly NFL quarterback Peyton Manning,

00:24:04.639 --> 00:24:06.021
that would be him.

00:24:06.021 --> 00:24:08.141
And second of all I can't
give him a B because of how

00:24:08.141 --> 00:24:09.666
badly he's doing in class.

00:24:09.666 --> 00:24:13.404
Let me read you his essay
about a tale of two cities.

00:24:13.404 --> 00:24:15.992
"Help, please help me,
call the police, I'm Peyton

00:24:15.992 --> 00:24:19.750
"Manning and I've been kidnapped
by the lady from Speed."

00:24:19.750 --> 00:24:22.448
- Then that's it, we're sunk.

00:24:22.448 --> 00:24:24.546
- We're sunk, no you're sunk.

00:24:24.546 --> 00:24:26.757
Because you see I've contacted
the authorities and they're,

00:24:26.757 --> 00:24:28.997
oh my God you've poisoned my coffee.

00:24:28.997 --> 00:24:30.589
- [Narrator Two] See the film Archie

00:24:30.589 --> 00:24:31.950
Manning called "terrifying."

00:24:31.950 --> 00:24:34.683
Tony Dungy says "it's spine tingling."

00:24:34.683 --> 00:24:37.761
And Bill Belichick raves "it's hilarious."

00:24:37.761 --> 00:24:40.311
- We want to know if
you would like to become

00:24:40.311 --> 00:24:41.813
part of this family.

00:24:41.813 --> 00:24:43.914
- Let me ask your kids something, did she

00:24:43.914 --> 00:24:45.497
kidnap you as well?

00:24:47.659 --> 00:24:51.826
And if I say no something
terrible's going to happen to me?

00:24:53.972 --> 00:24:55.736
So I guess my best bet
is to just pretend I'm a

00:24:55.736 --> 00:24:58.069
teenager even though I'm 34.

00:25:01.793 --> 00:25:03.486
Fine, I'm in.

00:25:03.486 --> 00:25:04.664
Let's do it.

00:25:04.664 --> 00:25:06.160
(laughing)

00:25:06.160 --> 00:25:07.243
You okay kid?

00:25:08.167 --> 00:25:09.818
Everything okay in there?

00:25:09.818 --> 00:25:11.754
(evil laughing)

00:25:11.754 --> 00:25:14.489
- [Narrator Two] The dark side.

00:25:14.489 --> 00:25:16.508
- How's he doing.

00:25:16.508 --> 00:25:18.613
- Hasn't quite gotten the hang of it yet.

00:25:18.613 --> 00:25:20.335
- That's because I'm
playing offensive line,

00:25:20.335 --> 00:25:21.835
I'm a quarterback!

00:25:23.953 --> 00:25:24.870
Sorry Mama.

00:25:25.928 --> 00:25:27.519
- [Narrator Two] In theaters never.

00:25:27.519 --> 00:25:29.830
- Hey, you're Peyton Manning.

